  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on self-reported perceptions of the urban environment rather than purely objective spatial measurements to determine travel behavior associations.",
      "what": "Using a survey of nearly 5,000 respondents across Helsinki, Copenhagen, London, Munich, and Las Palmas de Gran Canaria, this study analyzed how the perceived urban environment influences active travel decisions. Binary logit models, controlled for objective built environment metrics in home neighborhoods, evaluated how these perceptions affect trip-making, mode choice, and route choice. The results show that the urban environment has a stronger association with mode and route choices than with the initial decision to make a trip, with the strongest and most consistent effects observed among highly educated respondents and those with children.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that the influence of the built environment on active travel is not uniform, showing that socio-demographic associations vary significantly by city. This undercuts the effectiveness of universal, one-size-fits-all urban transport strategies that plan for an average citizen or city."
    }
